"SetNamedSecurityInfoW function (aclapi.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-setnamedsecurityinfow)\nciteturn39view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/aclapi/nf-aclapi-setnamedsecurityinfow\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # SetNamedSecurityInfoW function (aclapi.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: The SetNamedSecurityInfo function sets specified security information in the 【5†security descriptor】 of a specified object. The caller identifies the object by name.\nL33: ## Syntax\nL34: \nL35:     `DWORD SetNamedSecurityInfoW(\nL36:       [in]           LPWSTR               pObjectName,\nL37:       [in]           SE_OBJECT_TYPE       ObjectType,\nL38:       [in]           SECURITY_INFORMATION SecurityInfo,\nL39:       [in, optional] PSID                 psidOwner,\nL40:       [in, optional] PSID                 psidGroup,\nL41:       [in, optional] PACL                 pDacl,\nL42:       [in, optional] PACL                 pSacl\nL43:     );\nL44:     `\nL45: ## Parameters\nL46: \nL47: `[in] pObjectName`\nL48: \nL49: A pointer to a null-terminated string that specifies the name of the object for which to set security information. This can be the name of a local or remote file or directory on an NTFS file system, network share, registry key, semaphore, event, mutex, file mapping, or waitable timer.\nL50: \nL51: For descriptions of the string formats for the different object types, see 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】.\nL52: \nL53: `[in] ObjectType`\nL54: A value of the 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】 enumeration that indicates the type of object named by the pObjectName parameter.\nL55: \nL56: `[in] SecurityInfo`\nL57: \nL58: A set of bit flags that indicate the type of security information to set. This parameter can be a combination of the 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】 bit flags.\nL59: \nL60: `[in, optional] psidOwner`\nL61: A pointer to a 【8†SID】 structure that identifies the owner of the object. If the caller does not have the SeRestorePrivilege constant (see 【9†Privilege Constants】), this SID must be contained in the caller's token, and must have the SE_GROUP_OWNER permission enabled. The SecurityInfo parameter must include the OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. To set the owner, the caller must have WRITE_OWNER access to the object or have the SE_TAKE_OWNERSHIP_NAME privilege enabled.\nL62: If you are not setting the owner SID, this parameter can be NULL.\nL63: `[in, optional] psidGroup`\nL64: \nL65: A pointer to a SID that identifies the primary group of the object. The SecurityInfo parameter must include the GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. If you are not setting the primary group SID, this parameter can be NULL.\nL66: \nL67: `[in, optional] pDacl`\nL68: A pointer to the new DACL for the object. The SecurityInfo parameter must include the DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION flag. The caller must have WRITE_DAC access to the object or be the owner of the object. If you are not setting the DACL, this parameter can be NULL.\nL69: \nL70: `[in, optional] pSacl`\nL71: A pointer to the new SACL for the object. The SecurityInfo parameter must include any of the following flags: SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION, LABEL_SECURITY_INFORMATION, ATTRIBUTE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, SCOPE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, or BACKUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION.\nL72: \nL73: If setting SACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION or SCOPE_SECURITY_INFORMATION, the caller must have the SE_SECURITY_NAME privilege enabled. If you are not setting the SACL, this parameter can be NULL.\nL74: ## Return value\nL75: \nL76: If the function succeeds, the function returns ERROR_SUCCESS.\nL77: \nL78: If the function fails, it returns a nonzero error code defined in WinError.h.\nL79: ## Remarks\nL80: \nL81: If you are setting the 【10†discretionary access control list】 (DACL) or any elements in the 【5†system access control list】 (SACL) of an object, the system automatically propagates any inheritable 【11†access control entries】 (ACEs) to existing child objects, according to the 【12†rules of inheritance】.\nL82: \nL83: You can use the SetNamedSecurityInfo function with the following types of objects:\nL84:   * Local or remote files or directories on an NTFS\nL85:   * Local or remote printers\nL86:   * Local or remote Windows services\nL87:   * Network shares\nL88:   * Registry keys\nL89:   * Semaphores, events, mutexes, and waitable timers\nL90:   * File-mapping objects\nL91:   * Directory service objects\nL92: The SetNamedSecurityInfo function does not reorder access-allowed or access-denied ACEs based on the preferred order. When propagating inheritable ACEs to existing child objects, SetNamedSecurityInfo puts inherited ACEs in order after all of the noninherited ACEs in the DACLs of the child objects.\nL93: This function transfers information in 【13†plaintext】. The information transferred by this function is signed unless signing has been turned off for the system, but no encryption is performed.\nL94: \nL95: When you update access rights of a folder indicated by an UNC path, for example \\Test\\TestFolder, the original inherited ACE is removed and the full volume path is not included.\nL96: #### Examples\nL97: \nL98: For an example that uses this function, see 【14†Modifying the ACLs of an Object】 or 【15†Taking Object Ownership】.\nL99: \nL100: Note\nL101: The aclapi.h header defines SetNamedSecurityInfo as an alias that automatically selects the ANSI or Unicode version of this function based on the definition of the UNICODE preprocessor constant. Mixing usage of the encoding-neutral alias with code that is not encoding-neutral can lead to mismatches that result in compilation or runtime errors. For more information, see 【16†Conventions for Function Prototypes】.\nL102: ## Requirements\nL103: \nL104: Requirement  | Value\nL105: --- | ---\nL106: Minimum supported client  | Windows XP [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL107: Minimum supported server  | Windows Server 2003 [desktop apps | UWP apps]\nL108: Target Platform  | Windows\nL109: Header  | aclapi.h\nL110: Library  | Advapi32.lib\nL111: DLL  | Advapi32.dll\nL112: ## See also\nL113: \nL114: 【17†ACL】\nL115: \nL116: 【18†Access Control】\nL117: \nL118: 【19†Basic Access Control Functions】\nL119: \nL120: 【20†GetNamedSecurityInfo】\nL121: \nL122: 【21†GetSecurityInfo】\nL123: \nL124: 【22†SECURITY_DESCRIPTOR】\nL125: \nL126: 【7†SECURITY_INFORMATION】\nL127: \nL128: 【6†SE_OBJECT_TYPE】\nL129: \nL130: 【8†SID】\nL131: \nL132: 【23†SetSecurityInfo】\nL133: \nL134: * * *\nL135: \nL136: ## Feedback\nL137: \nL138: Was this page helpful?\nL139: \nL140: Yes No No\nL141: \nL142: Need help with this topic?\nL143: \nL144: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL145: \nL146: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL147: \nL148: Suggest a fix?\nL149: \nL150: * * *\nL151: \nL152: ## Additional resources\nL153: \nL154: * * *\nL155: \nL156:   * Last updated on 2023-02-09\nL157: ### In this article\nL158: \nL159: Was this page helpful?\nL160: \nL161: Need help with this topic?\nL162: \nL163: Want 